WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.220
- [Narrator] If you've
ever been within earshot

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.070
of a Spanish soccer broadcast,
(audience cheering)

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.192
you've probably heard this yell.

00:00:06.192 --> 00:00:09.346
(yelling in foreign language)

00:00:09.346 --> 00:00:11.340
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.480
The reflexive, pulse-pounding howl

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.089
that either lifts your spirits,

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:16.870
(audience cheering)
or drags it down.

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:18.700
- [Andrés] It brings out a lot of emotion,

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.680
because it's the most important,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.140
and perhaps decisive, play of the game.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:23.973
- [Narrator] The goal call

00:00:23.973 --> 00:00:25.990
is an undeniable staple of soccer,

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:27.430
and no one knows this more

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.651
than Telemundo play-by-play
announcer, Andrés Cantor.

00:00:30.651 --> 00:00:31.484
(inhaling loudly)

00:00:31.484 --> 00:00:32.890
(yelling)

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.330
This is the story of the voice

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.163
that took the U.S. by storm.

00:00:43.619 --> 00:00:46.810
Decades before Cantor
even got close to a mic,

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:48.120
he was living in Argentina

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.263
looking for a big break on the pitch.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.820
- I actually wanted to
play soccer professionally.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.120
I tried out in the minor
leagues, then, I came to the U.S.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.930
Played high school, played college,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.790
and the joke is that here in the U.S.,

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:05.080
the professional league wasn't around,

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.480
so I didn't have anywhere to go,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.640
and I decided to the next best thing,

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.850
which is broadcasting.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.360
- [Narrator] Fast-forward
a few years later.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.220
- [Andrés] I got the call from Univision,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.870
they asked me to do play-by-play.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.000
- [Narrator] Cantor's lively
commentary was infectious.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.980
- [Andrés] I'm really, really
passionate about soccer,

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.760
and I think that transcends
into my broadcast,

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.063
and they hired me within 48 hours.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:27.680
♪ Soccer, yeah ♪

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.770
- [Narrator] As Cantor did
more and more broadcasts,

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.100
soccer was becoming mainstream in the U.S.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.290
Hitting a fever pitch in 1994,

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.218
when it hosted the World Cup.

00:01:36.218 --> 00:01:37.051
(whistle blowing)

00:01:37.051 --> 00:01:39.300
- I had the opportunity
of calling all matches

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.560
of that World Cup, and that
created a lot of pressure

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.490
on myself.
(whistle blowing)

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.690
- [Narrator] But Cantor never
buckled under the pressure,

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:48.285
and started attracting new fans.

00:01:48.285 --> 00:01:49.841
(announcer yelling in foreign language)

00:01:49.841 --> 00:01:50.674
- [Andrés] And I remember

00:01:50.674 --> 00:01:52.290
that after the first couple of games,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.540
my work started getting attention.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.690
- [Narrator] The way he called the games

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.270
was unlike anything many
English-speaking Americans

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.183
had heard before, especially
when someone scored.

00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:03.776
- Goal!

00:02:05.989 --> 00:02:06.822
(whistle blowing)

00:02:06.822 --> 00:02:08.050
Well, basically, in Latin America

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.980
we always have called the
goal call the same way.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.090
Here in the U.S., I just was
very lucky to be in a position

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.410
where I helped popularized
a little bit the game

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.470
through my broadcast.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:21.880
- [Narrator] Though Cantor didn't invent

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.560
the goal battle cry he
certainly perfected it,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.740
and soccer fans, old and new
alike, couldn't get enough.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.520
- Please welcome
Univision's Andrés Cantor.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:31.496
Andrés!
(dramatic music)

00:02:31.496 --> 00:02:32.390
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.610
- I was very fortunate, after 1994,

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.515
that my career received so much attention.

00:02:38.515 --> 00:02:39.348
No!

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.700
I started doing many endorsements
for different companies

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.914
and had cameo appearances
in television shows.

00:02:47.914 --> 00:02:48.814
I have no control!

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:56.343
This job has taken its toll!

00:02:56.343 --> 00:02:57.880
(upbeat music)

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.450
- [Narrator] But despite
these opportunities,

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.070
Cantor hasn't lost sight of
why he's still doing this

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.340
30 years later.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.680
- I mean, soccer has been my life since

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.410
I can remember, really.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:11.000
I really feel very
passionate about the game,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.910
and I bring on that passion from

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:16.243
the minute the whistle blows
to the end of the game.

